<h2>Three species of bail SLP</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Against refusal.</strong> The accused, having failed before the Sessions Court and the High Court, seeks liberty from the final court. The petition&rsquo;s core is the specific case for bail — role, parity, custody period, trial progress — and the High Court&rsquo;s failure to engage with it.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Against grant.</strong> The State or the informant contends the High Court granted bail unsustainably — overlooking the material, the antecedents, or statutory bars. These petitions succeed where the order reads as conclusory; reasoned discretion is rarely disturbed.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Against cancellation, or seeking it.</strong> Supervening misconduct — threatening witnesses, violating conditions, influencing the trial — is pursued first before the court that granted bail; Article 136 then reviews how that court dealt with it.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the Court looks for</h2>
<p>Across all three species, the examination is structural. Did the court below identify the correct factors — nature and gravity of the accusation, the evidence, severity of punishment, danger of absconding, character of the material witnesses and the risk of their being influenced, and the larger interest of the public and State? Did it apply any statutory overlay — the stringent twin conditions of special statutes, or the restrictions on successive applications? Did it record reasons connecting those factors to the result? Orders that show this architecture survive; orders that substitute adjectives for analysis do not. The Court has also been consistently attentive to one systemic reality: undertrial custody that stretches across years while trial crawls erodes the presumption of innocence, and bail petitions grounded in that delay — with a precise chronology of the custody and the trial&rsquo;s stagnation — command serious attention.</p>

<h2>Building the petition</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The chronology is the case.</strong> Date of FIR, arrest, chargesheet, each bail application and order, the trial&rsquo;s progress witness by witness: bail SLPs are won on precise chronologies far more often than on rhetoric.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Confront the record.</strong> Annex every bail order below, the status reports, and any order recording conduct on interim protection. Suppression of an earlier adverse order is fatal and invites costs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Frame interim relief deliberately.</strong> For refusals: interim bail pending the petition, supported by medical or custody-length grounds where they exist. For grants: a stay application that explains why liberty should be interrupted before hearing — a high bar.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Anticipate conditions.</strong> The Court frequently resolves bail SLPs by calibrated conditions — surrender of passport, reporting, trial-cooperation undertakings, time-bound trial directions — and counsel ready with workable conditions often convert a doubtful petition into a conditional order.</div>
</div>

<h2>What does not travel</h2>
<p>Petitions that re-argue the facts of the FIR, that treat the SLP as a third bail application on merits, or that seek to relitigate concurrent discretionary refusals without identifying any misdirection, are routinely dismissed at the first hearing — sometimes with the observation that liberty of the subject does not convert every bail order into a question of law of general importance. Equally, the State&rsquo;s petitions against bail that rest on gravity alone, where the High Court&rsquo;s order shows consideration of the record, rarely cross the leave stage. Article 136 is a corrective for flawed decision-making about bail; it is not an additional tier of bail jurisdiction.</p>
<div class="note">Bail below is now governed by Sections 480 to 483 of the BNSS, 2023 (formerly Sections 437 to 439 CrPC), with anticipatory bail under Section 482 BNSS. In criminal SLPs where the petitioner stands convicted, the separate surrender requirements of the Supreme Court Rules apply before the petition is entertained, with exemption available on application. This article is general information about practice under Article 136 and is not advice in any matter.</div>
